module std.bytes

import std.types { Bytes }
import std.integer { UInt8, QualifiedOctets, qualified_octets }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable() -> Int {
  1 / 0
}

fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable_float() -> Float {
  if pure_dag_seam_unreachable() == 0 { 0.0 } else { 0.0 }
}

// One divergent seam, projected once per result type, because a divergent expression HAS no result
// type to project from. `1 / 0` never returns, so the honest type is bottom — the type that
// inhabits every type — and .dag has no bottom, so the seam is typed at whatever it is used as.
// Typing it Int alone was a silent lie wherever the enclosing declaration was not Int: `fn ... ->
// List<Float> { [pure_dag_seam_unreachable()] }` in the simd and wgsl float kernels declared
// List<Float> and produced List<Int>, and the declaration was the correct half — those are float
// kernels taking List<Float> arguments. The declared-type conformance wall found both by execution
// once it was widened to containers of ground kernel scalars; before that the gate required a plain
// shape and a List is not one, so the mismatch was indistinguishable from a conforming declaration
// (codex review 45398). The bodies are unreachable, so nothing MISBEHAVED — which is exactly why
// this had to be caught by construction rather than by a consumer. THE FLOAT PROJECTION MUST BORROW
// THE INT SEAM'S DIVERGENCE, and the first version did not — it was written `1.0 / 0.0`, which
// under IEEE-754 is not a trap at all but positive infinity, so the seam RETURNED and the kernels
// asserted to be unreachable would have produced [+inf]. That is a fabricated plausible output, the
// precise thing §5 forbids, introduced while fixing a different fabrication (codex review 45493).
// Integer division by zero traps; float division by zero does not, and the two do not have the same
// failure semantics however similar they look. The Float projection therefore forces the Int seam
// to evaluate in its condition, so divergence happens before any Float is produced; both arms are
// the same literal because neither is ever reached, and the literal is there to give the expression
// its type, not to be a value. The two fns are a §2 duplication and are deliberately marked as one:
// dissolve-on a bottom type, at which point one divergent seam inhabits every result type and the
// Float projection deletes.

// The String projection of the same divergence, added for the same reason the Float one exists and
// borrowing the Int seam's divergence the same way: a caller whose result type is String and whose
// refusal arm is unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION has nowhere to put a value, and "" is a perfectly good
// String, which is exactly what makes it a fabricated plausible output. The condition forces the
// Int seam to evaluate, so divergence happens before any String is produced; both arms are the same
// literal because neither is ever reached. DISSOLVE-ON: the same bottom type the Float projection
// names -- one divergent seam inhabits every result type and both projections delete.
fn pure_dag_seam_unreachable_string() -> String {
  if pure_dag_seam_unreachable() == 0 { "" } else { "" }
}

fn bytes_octets(b: Bytes) -> List<UInt8> {
  [pure_dag_seam_unreachable()]
}

// THE BYTES MINT FOR QualifiedOctets, AND IT IS A CONSTRUCTOR RATHER THAN A CHECK ON PURPOSE.
//
// std.encoding base64_encode declares the SEALED carrier QualifiedOctets -- the proof that every
// member passed uint8_of_int admission -- and the only general route to one is std.integer's
// uint8_octets_of_ints fold, which returns a Result. For a value that arrives from BYTES that
// fold's refusal arm is UNREACHABLE, and THE RANGE IS A DECLARED CONTRACT RATHER THAN AN APPEAL TO
// WHAT A BYTE IS: gunbc.primitive_egress.dispositions_text carries the bytes_octets evidence row,
// whose result is "List<Int> of octets 0..255; total". So the scan can only ever answer Absent,
// and every caller would be forced to write a refusal branch no input can reach. The distinction
// matters because this mint rests on that contract: bytes_octets' own .dag body is the divergent
// seam, so unlike word_to_octets -- whose members are computed in-corpus through uint8_of_int --
// the range here is established by the declared row and not by a computation this module can see.
// THAT DEPENDENCY IS WALLED RATHER THAN ASSERTED HERE. If the row's result stops saying 0..255
// this admission stops holding and the mint owes the fold -- and in prose alone that sentence
// would be an obligation on whoever happens to read it, which DESIGN section 4c forbids treating
// as evidence, since no Accepted program can read an annotation. The join between this mint's
// assumption and the declared row is therefore a claim:
// test.claim.bytes_qualified_octets_range_contract_witness_test, which reds if the row moves. std.integer names that trap in its own roster annotation -- word_to_octets is admitted to
// the mint precisely because "routing it through the fold would add a refusal arm no input can
// reach" -- and DESIGN section 5 names it more generally: a check that restates a constraint the
// model already carries is validation standing where construction was available.
//
// So this is the SECOND instance of the case that roster entry already established, not a new
// kind of exception. It is admitted on the same reasoning and its shape satisfies the same
// discipline: the mint must have no formal a caller can pre-load with unproved members. This takes
// a Bytes -- exactly as word_to_octets takes a Word and a width -- so the proved list and the
// minted list are the same value, derived here, and no caller can hand in a member list at all.
// A caller holding raw Ints still has only the fold; nothing here widens that route.
fn bytes_qualified_octets(b: Bytes) -> QualifiedOctets {
  qualified_octets(members: bytes_octets(b: b))
}

fn octets_bytes(o: List<UInt8>) -> Bytes {
  o as Bytes
}

fn utf8_encode_bytes(s: String) -> Bytes {
  s as Bytes
}

data bytes_seam_host_realization_marker: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "std.bytes",
    decl_name: "builtin_function_registry",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}
